           <ul class="steps">
             <li><strong>Passive filtering.</strong> A fixed ring routes existing light according to wavelength. Sweep the probe with zero drive to see the through notch and drop peak. Input power scales the output in this linear regime.</li>
+            <li><a href="opa-roadmap.html#opa-m">OPA-M research plan</a> applies receiver and conversion accounting to a digital memory link.</li>
             <li><strong>Electro-optic logic.</strong> Chip 11 detects A and B electrically and assumes a silicon carrier-depletion resonance shift of 130 pm/V. Coupled-mode theory models intrinsic and coupling loss. The ideal symmetric combiner adds complex fields with matched external path phases by default. The phase-step control adds a relative phase ramp across the four paths. Its unused ports carry the rest of the selected power. Loss of phase match, electrical bandwidth and noise require separate characterization.</li>
             <li><strong>All-optical nonlinear logic.</strong> An optical control can shift a resonance through Kerr, free-carrier or thermal effects. Each mechanism has its own dynamics and loss. This mode would need an optical control path, a calibrated nonlinear model and independent power, speed and regeneration tests.</li>
