 <div class="grid"><div class="stat"><small>OPA 0.1</small><strong>Optical experiment</strong><small>USB or LAN control is sufficient</small></div><div class="stat"><small>OPA 0.2</small><strong>Digital API</strong><small>DAC/ADC + FPGA or MCU</small></div><div class="stat"><small>OPA 1.0</small><strong>PCIe accelerator</strong><small>After validation of a useful workload</small></div></div>
+<section class="card" id="opa-m" aria-labelledby="opa-m-roadmap-title">
+  <h2 id="opa-m-roadmap-title">OPA-M · Open Photonic Memory Interconnect</h2>
+  <p>The near-term question is where extra link bandwidth changes inference performance. The long-term goal is an open, fabricable digital link between electronic memory and an AI accelerator.</p>
+  <p><strong>Current evidence:</strong> Chip 12 is a teaching simulation. OPA-M-001 adds reproducible analytical comparisons with matched payload and an independent limit on how fast the accelerator can accept data. Electronic measurements for calibration remain open. Physical link validation is a later milestone.</p>
+  <ol>
+    <li><strong>OPA-M-001 · AI Inference and Memory Bottleneck Modeling</strong> — active, with analytical results for dense and sparse workloads.</li>
+    <li><strong>OPA-M-002 · Parallel Memory Bank Architecture</strong> — planned. Compare sustainable bandwidth from DDR, LPDDR, GDDR and HBM.</li>
+    <li><strong>OPA-M-003 · Single-Channel Optical Memory Link</strong> — planned. Check receiver margin, bit error rate, latency and energy.</li>
+    <li><strong>OPA-M-004 · WDM Photonic Memory Interconnect</strong> — planned. Evaluate 8, 16, 32, 64 and 128 wavelengths against a one-channel reference.</li>
+    <li><strong>OPA-M-005 · Photonic Physical Layout</strong> — planned. Select a process, check design rules and design passive and single-channel structures for testing.</li>
+    <li><strong>OPA-M-006 · Electronic and Photonic Integration</strong> — planned. Start with a memory-controller and FPGA loopback.</li>
+    <li><strong>OPA-M-007 · AI Inference Software Integration</strong> — planned. Measure prefetch, caching and expert traffic with matched workloads.</li>
+    <li><strong>OPA-M-008 · Physical Prototype and Experimental Validation</strong> — planned. Measure a one-lane prototype before testing WDM and inference.</li>
+  </ol>
+  <p><a href="./#opa-ecosystem">OPA Ecosystem</a> · <a href="./#photonic-circuit-lab/photonic-memory">Chip 12</a> · <a href="https://github.com/cryptomanxxx/debatt-ai-open-photonic-lab/blob/main/research/OPA-M-ROADMAP.md">Research dependencies and verification gates</a> · <a href="https://github.com/cryptomanxxx/debatt-ai-open-photonic-lab/blob/main/research/OPA-M-001.md">OPA-M-001 methods and sources</a></p>
+</section>
 <section class="card"><h2>Physical signal path for OPA 0.1</h2><div class="flow"><div>Host computer · Python and test vector</div><span>↓</span><div>Driver electronics and DAC for settings and signal modulation</div><span>↓</span><div>1,550 nm light source and optical coupling → packaged TFLN PIC</div><span>↓</span><div>Detection with photodiodes, amplifiers, and ADC</div><span>↓</span><div>Calibration and comparison with a CPU-based reference</div></div><p class="warning">A standard photodiode measures intensity. Recovering the sign or complex amplitude requires a readout method such as balanced or coherent detection, or an encoding scheme that maps the quantity of interest to intensity.</p></section>
 <section class="card"><h2>Fabrication and procurement</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Subsystem</th><th>Proposed approach</th><th>Requirements before ordering</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Photonic chip</td><td>Custom GDSFactory design with an MPW run at a European foundry</td><td class="state">DRC, optical I/O, test structures, and design acceptance</td></tr><tr><td>Optical laser and fiber network</td><td>Off-the-shelf C-band components for the lab prototype</td><td class="state">Wavelength, power, polarization, coupling type, and laser safety</td></tr><tr><td>Photonic packaging</td><td>Professional fiber attachment, die bonding, and wire bonding</td><td class="state">Quote and packaging rules before the chip layout is finalized</td></tr><tr><td>Detectors and analog electronics</td><td>Off-the-shelf photoreceivers, TIAs, and ADC/DAC evaluation boards</td><td class="state">Accuracy, dynamic range, bandwidth, and coherent readout</td></tr><tr><td>Digital control</td><td>MCU or FPGA with open Python software</td><td class="state">Interface selection and measurable performance</td></tr><tr><td>PCIe and server</td><td>Custom PCIe card and a standard Linux host in a later phase</td><td class="state">After OPA 0.2 demonstrates a useful workload</td></tr></tbody></table></section>
